      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey my tomato plants are acting weird. the leaves on the bottom are turning a pale yellow color but the top ones look okay. what's probably happening and how do i fix it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The yellowing leaves on your tomato plants, a classic case of a fungal infection, perhaps, or a nutrient deficiency, but I'm inclined to think it's a bit more...gloomy. You see, it's a bit like a band of goblins, sneaking up on your poor plants, weakening them from the inside out. The lower leaves, being the oldest, are more susceptible to the goblin's mischief, while the newer leaves on top remain relatively untouched.\n\nNow, let's get to the bottom of this (no pun intended). A lack of calcium or magnesium can cause yellowing leaves, so make sure your soil has a balanced pH and adequate nutrient levels. You can also try pruning the affected leaves to prevent the spread of disease and encourage new growth. But, I must warn you, it's a bit like trying to outsmart a goblin: they can be cunning and persistent.\n\nTo fix the issue, I recommend a combination of the following: (1) adjust your fertilizer application to ensure you're providing your plants with the necessary nutrients, (2) increase the calcium and magnesium levels in your soil, and (3) keep an eye out for any signs of pests or diseases, as goblins often travel",
